benchmark: zengif find_nearest branchless chunked-min optimization (ARM A/B) — FALSIFIED
date: 2026-05-29
hostname: Hetzner cax21 (arm-zen-ubuntu), ARM Neoverse-N1 aarch64, 4 cores, 7.5 GB RAM
toolchain: cargo 1.96.0 (30a34c682 2026-05-25)
rustflags: '' (runtime SIMD dispatch; NO target-cpu=native)
build: cargo build --release -j2 --bench codec
bench_cmd: target/release/deps/codec-* --bench   (zenbench criterion-compat, all groups)

method:
  ONE source tree, src/types.rs swapped between two versions and rebuilt
  (each phase forced to recompile; 17.65s baseline / 17.93s optimized):
    BASELINE  scalar `if dist < best_dist` scan   (on-box marker chunks_exact=0 best_dist=3)
    OPTIMIZED branchless chunked (dist<<8)|idx min (on-box marker chunks_exact=1 best_dist=1)
  Optimized file integrity verified by md5 local<->box: 68f52c4372126e5aeb2e2102c33a139c
  CAVEAT: zenbench printed "0 rounds" (single-shot, no statistical rounds). Each
  number is one measurement. The regression magnitude (~30%) is far outside the
  decode-control noise band (<=3%) and consistent across both solid encode paths,
  so the verdict stands; the marginal animation wins (2-6%) are within the
  single-shot noise and not claimed as a reliable improvement.

measured_result (zenbench mean, REAL numbers from zengif_arm_bench_FINAL.log):
  encode/solid/256x256x10            12.6ms -> 16.5ms    0.76x  REGRESSION (-31%)
  encode/solid/512x512x10            54.5ms -> 70.9ms    0.77x  REGRESSION (-30%)
  memory_allocation/encode           231.4ms -> 310.2ms  0.76x  REGRESSION (-34%)
  animation_stress/encode dirty32   517.5ms -> 504.4ms   1.026x marginal
  animation_stress/encode dirty256  3545.6ms -> 3348.9ms 1.059x marginal
  decode (control, no find_nearest): unchanged within noise (<=3%)

VERDICT: NOT A WIN on ARM. The optimization regresses the dominant encode/solid
and memory_allocation paths ~30% while giving only a marginal (single-shot,
noise-band) improvement on dirty-region animation encode. REVERTED on main.

root_cause_hypothesis:
  Solid / near-solid frames hit an exact palette match at a LOW index. The scalar
  loop's per-element `dist==0` early-exit returns after ~1 comparison on those
  pixels. The chunked-min must process a full 8-entry chunk before it can early-
  exit (the `(best>>8)==0` check is only between chunks), so it does MORE work on
  the common case. The isolated x86 microbench (grad+full256 1.121x, few+exact
  1.172x, grad+exact256 1.166x) used palettes/pixels with few index-0 exact hits,
  so it did not surface this. A future attempt should preserve a cheap per-element
  exact-match exit (scalar prefix or index-0 probe) before the vector body, then
  re-bench on ARM.

correctness (still valid, unaffected by the revert):
  find_nearest output was byte-identical baseline vs optimized across all corpus
  files (benchmarks/zengif_find_nearest_correctness_2026-05-29.tsv); 162 tests
  passed. The revert restores the scalar baseline, so output is unchanged.

x86_local_microbench (7950X, isolated find_nearest loop, runtime dispatch):
  grad+full256 1.121x | few+exact 1.172x | grad+exact256 1.166x | 0 mismatches
  (did NOT predict the ARM end-to-end regression — kept here as a caution.)
